<HEAD>§ 4.72 Metric standards of fill.</HEAD>
<P>(a) <I>Authorized standards of fill.</I> The standards of fill for wine are the following:</P>
<P>(1) 3 liters.</P>
<P>(2) 2.25 liters.</P>
<P>(3) 1.8 liters.</P>
<P>(4) 1.5 liters.</P>
<P>(5) 1 liter.</P>
<P>(6) 750 milliliters.</P>
<P>(7) 720 milliliters.</P>
<P>(8) 700 milliliters.</P>
<P>(9) 620 milliliters.</P>
<P>(10) 600 milliliters.</P>
<P>(11) 568 milliliters.</P>
<P>(12) 550 milliliters.</P>
<P>(13) 500 milliliters.</P>
<P>(14) 473 milliliters.</P>
<P>(15) 375 milliliters.</P>
<P>(16) 360 milliliters.</P>
<P>(17) 355 milliliters.</P>
<P>(18) 330 milliliters.</P>
<P>(19) 300 milliliters.</P>
<P>(20) 250 milliliters.</P>
<P>(21) 200 milliliters.</P>
<P>(22) 187 milliliters.</P>
<P>(23) 180 milliliters.</P>
<P>(24) 100 milliliters.</P>
<P>(25) 50 milliliters.</P>
<P>(b) <I>Sizes larger than 3 liters.</I> Wine may be bottled or packed in containers of 4 liters or larger if the containers are filled and labeled in quantities of even liters (4 liters, 5 liters, 6 liters, etc.).</P>
<P>(c) <I>Tolerances.</I> The tolerances in fill are the same as are allowed by § 4.37 in respect to statement of net contents on labels.</P>
